  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The framework is demonstrated on railway systems under rainfall, so its parameters may require recalibration for other infrastructure systems or extreme weather hazards.",
      "what": "The study introduces a coupled dynamic framework that models transportation networks as complex systems interacting with external spatial fields, using real-world railway networks exposed to rainfall as a primary case study. The model identifies a critical threshold in rainfall intensity where local disruptions transition into systemic transportation collapse. It introduces metrics for system perturbability and societal risk to map structural vulnerabilities across the broader rail infrastructure under localized environmental perturbations.",
      "why": "Instead of treating weather disruptions as uniform or purely localized events, this framework quantifies the exact threshold at which weather perturbations induce cascading system-wide collapse in transport networks."
    }
  },
